3. A company uses AWS Organizations to centrally manage multiple AWS accounts for different departments. 
The management account owns an Amazon S3 bucket that stores project reports.
The company wants to restrict access to this S3 bucket so that only users from accounts within the same AWS Organization can access it, while also aiming to minimize operational overhead.

Goal: Ensure that only accounts within the AWS Organization can access the S3 bucket, with minimal operational overhead.

A. Add the aws:PrincipalOrgID global condition key with a reference to the organization ID to the S3 bucket policy.

aws:PrincipalOrgID is a global condition key that can be used in resource-based policies like S3 bucket policies.

It allows access only to principals (IAM users, roles, etc.) that belong to accounts within a specific AWS Organization.

This approach requires no per-account, per-user, or per-tag management, making it the most scalable and low-maintenance solution.

Example S3 Bucket Policy:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-org-bucket",
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-org-bucket/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalOrgID": "o-1234567890"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

Why not the other options?

B. aws:PrincipalOrgPaths – Requires managing Organizational Units (OUs) and updating the policy if OUs change; more complex.
C. CloudTrail-based approach – Reactive and manual; introduces significant overhead.
D. aws:PrincipalTag – Requires tagging each user and managing those tags, which adds operational burden.



4. An application runs on an Amazon EC2 instance in a VPC. 
   The application processes logs that are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. 
   The EC2 instance needs to access the S3 bucket without connectivity to the internet.
   Which solution will provide private network connectivity to Amazon S3?

A. Create a gateway VPC endpoint to the S3 bucket.
B. Stream the logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Export the logs to the S3 bucket.
C. Create an instance profile on Amazon EC2 to allow S3 access.
D. Create an Amazon API Gateway API with a private link to access the S3 endpoint

Goal: The EC2 instance must connect without internet access
A: A gateway VPC endpoint is a private connection between a VPC and AWS services (like S3 or DynamoDB), without needing an internet gateway, NAT gateway, or VPN.
It allows EC2 instances in private subnets to securely access S3 over the AWS network, without traversing the public internet.
This solution is fully managed, scalable, and adds no cost for data transfer between the VPC and S3 (within the same region).

VPC Gateway Endpoint:
For S3 and DynamoDB only. Provides scalable, fault-tolerant private access inside a VPC.

VPC Interface Endpoint (PrivateLink):
Used for most other AWS services (SNS, SQS, KMS, etc.), via an ENI in the subnet.

NAT Gateway vs VPC Endpoint:
NAT = enables internet-bound traffic from private subnets. Costly, and doesn’t satisfy the no internet requirement.
VPC Endpoint = secure, private, no internet involved.

Financial institutions: Banks often forbid direct internet connectivity from compute nodes. They rely on VPC endpoints for S3 to store transaction logs, while keeping workloads inside private subnets.
Healthcare companies: HIPAA-compliant architectures commonly use VPC endpoints for medical data storage in S3.



5. A company is hosting a web application on AWS using a single Amazon EC2 instance that stores user-uploaded documents in an Amazon EBS volume. 
   For better scalability and availability, the company duplicated the architecture and created a second EC2 instance and EBS volume in another Availability Zone, placing both behind an Application Load Balancer. 
   After completing this change, users reported that, each time they refreshed the website, they could see one subset of their documents or the other, but never all of the documents at the same time.
   What should a solutions architect propose to ensure users see all of their documents at once?

A. Copy the data so both EBS volumes contain all the documents
B. Configure the Application Load Balancer to direct a user to the server with the documents
C. Copy the data from both EBS volumes to Amazon EFS. Modify the application to save new documents to Amazon EFS
D. Configure the Application Load Balancer to send the request to both servers. Return each document from the correct server

Problem: Each server stores uploads on its own local EBS → users see different files depending on which server the load balancer routes them to.

C: Amazon EFS is a managed, elastic file system that can be mounted by multiple EC2 instances across multiple AZs.
With EFS: All servers read/write to the same shared file system. Users see a consistent set of documents regardless of which EC2 instance they land on.

EBS volumes are AZ-scoped and not shareable across AZs
EBS (Elastic Block Store): Block storage tied to a single AZ. Great for one instance, not for shared storage.
EFS (Elastic File System): Shared NFS file system, accessible from multiple EC2s across AZs. Scales automatically.
Stateless web design: Web/app tiers should be stateless. Shared data (like user uploads) should live in a common backend store (EFS or S3).

